content.thinking:
node-html-parser has a bug/quirk: with `pre: false` it drops the content of pre entirely. With default (pre:true), it keeps content as raw text. So the parser can't give us structured children inside `<pre>`.

Options:
1. In toTree, special-case: parse with default (pre as raw text), and for rawText block elements, recursively re-parse their text content? That's messy.
2. Change toTree to handle `pre` by recursively parsing its rawText content as HTML fragment.

Simplest robust approach: in walk(), if child is an element whose rawTagName is 'pre' (or in a set of block text elements), take its rawText and re-parse it with the same options and splice resulting nodes. Since sanitizer output inside pre is already clean, re-parsing is fine for verification purposes.

Implement:

```js
const RAW_TEXT_TAGS = new Set(['pre']); // parser hides nested elements here
function walk(node) {
  ...
  if (RAW_TEXT_TAGS.has(tag) ) {
    const inner = parse(child.rawText ?? '', {...same opts});
    out.push({tag, attrs, children: walk(inner)});
    continue;
  }
}
```

Note: node-html-parser element has `.rawText` getter; for pre with pre:true it returns raw text `<code>let x = 1</code>`. Good. But careful: rawText may be undefined for some nodes; guard.

Also V10, V12, V13 failures — need to check whether they're also caused by this or something else. svg/math: sanitize output 'okd' — test expects [{text:'ok'},{text:'d'}]. Hmm that should pass... unless failure was elsewhere. Let me fix toTree then rerun to see remaining diffs.

